    "topic_code": "3.4",
    "topic_title": "Sine and Cosine Function Graphs",
    "stem": "Which statement correctly describes the graph of the parent function y = sin x?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Its period is 2π, with a maximum at (0, 1) and a minimum at (π, −1).",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sine–cosine swap: these extrema belong to the parent cosine graph. The sine graph starts at (0, 0), not (0, 1). Fix: Mark each parent graph’s value at x = 0 before identifying its extrema."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Its period is 2π, with a maximum at (3π/2, 1) and a minimum at (π/2, −1).",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Maximum–minimum reversal: this switches the sine graph’s upper and lower extrema. Sine reaches 1 at π/2 and −1 at 3π/2. Fix: Sketch the five quarter-period anchor points before labeling the maximum and minimum."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Its period is 2π, with a maximum at (π/2, 1) and a minimum at (3π/2, −1).",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Over one cycle, the graph passes through (0, 0), (π/2, 1), (π, 0), (3π/2, −1), and (2π, 0). These anchor points show the maximum and minimum, and the full pattern repeats every 2π."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Its period is π, with a maximum at (π/2, 1) and a minimum at (3π/2, −1).",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Zero-spacing confusion: consecutive sine zeros are π apart, but that spacing is not a full period. From 0 to π the graph arches above the axis; from π to 2π it dips below. Fix: Trace until the entire graph pattern repeats, not just until the next zero."
     }
    ]
   },
